const phase6refs={4:'Cups or blocks are teacher-supplied; printable CUP cards are a flat alternative. Collect used question tokens visibly so limits remain fair.',8:'Role cards define information access. The Messenger carries words only and does not alter the design.',10:'Orthographic views show the same object from top, front, and side. Dashed lines indicate hidden edges only when the challenge uses them.',45:'All strips represent the same whole length. Compare fractions only after aligning equal wholes.',46:'Physical base-ten blocks are teacher-supplied. Ten of one place exchange for one of the next place; decimal use requires an explicitly labeled whole.',47:'A valid sort applies the same stated rule to every item. Some sets permit multiple defensible rules; record the chosen rule before comparison.',49:'Cards are paper alternatives to instruments. Follow classroom volume and instrument-safety expectations; a rest is intentional silence.',50:'Assume common time with four quarter-note beats per measure for these samples. Match notation depth to local music instruction.',58:'Use a clear, dry, obstacle-free walking area; no running, blindfolds, stairs, furniture climbing, or contact. A student may opt to move a token instead.',68:'A usable procedure states a goal, needed materials, ordered actions, measurable details, checks, and any relevant safety caution.',72:'Keep both sides of an equation balanced. Multiplication/division precede addition/subtraction unless grouping symbols change the order.',74:'Equivalent fractions name the same point/amount when the whole is the same size. Use strip alignment, circles, or a number line as evidence.'};
